# The format of this file is identical to `.dockerignore`.
# It is used to reduce the size of deployed images to make the process of publishing faster.

# No need to store the pnpm store twice.
.local

# --- generated build output (added 2026-08-06, CB-0 release provenance) ---
#
# `.gitignore` is NOT deployment-snapshot containment. Replit publishes the
# workspace FILESYSTEM, so a gitignored path still ships. Everything below is
# gitignored AND was being uploaded, then rebuilt from scratch by the deployment's
# own build step — so the uploaded copy was never used, only capable of confusing
# a future release about which output it was looking at.
#
# Each path is rebuilt by a build command declared in artifact configuration:
#   artifacts/isola/.next          <- `pnpm --filter @workspace/isola run build`
#                                     (artifacts/isola/.replit-artifact/artifact.toml)
#   artifacts/api-server/dist      <- `pnpm --filter @workspace/api-server run build`
#                                     (artifacts/api-server/.replit-artifact/artifact.toml),
#                                     which runs before `node artifacts/api-server/dist/index.mjs`;
#                                     scripts/run-replit-preview.sh also rebuilds it when absent.
#   artifacts/isola/.next-preview  <- `next dev` under the Preview workflow only
#                                     (NEXT_DIST_DIR in scripts/run-replit-preview.sh;
#                                     next.config.ts falls back to .next when unset,
#                                     so build/start/deployment are unchanged).
#
# This is a reproducibility fix, not the CB-0 root-cause fix. Source, Prisma
# schema and migrations, manifests, the lockfile, .replit-artifact/artifact.toml
# and source-controlled public assets are all deliberately still uploaded;
# artifacts/isola/lib/release/replitignore.test.ts asserts exactly that against
# this file.
#   *.tsbuildinfo                  <- TypeScript incremental state, written by
#                                     `tsc --build` (root `typecheck:libs`) and by
#                                     `tsc --noEmit` under "incremental": true.
#                                     It is not a build INPUT the release needs,
#                                     but `next build` type-checks, so a stale one
#                                     uploaded into the snapshot can change which
#                                     errors are re-checked. Exact paths, not a
#                                     glob: a tsbuildinfo appearing anywhere else
#                                     should stop the build for review rather than
#                                     be silently excluded.
artifacts/isola/.next
artifacts/isola/.next-preview
artifacts/api-server/dist
artifacts/isola/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
scripts/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# --- platform-created caches (added 2026-08-07, PR #83 real-build follow-up) ---
#
# Not build output — created by Replit's OWN platform steps ("Installing
# packages", "Running Security Scan") that run in the build container BEFORE the
# artifact's own build script (and therefore its preflight) ever executes. Two
# consecutive real Replit publish attempts on 2026-08-07 (builds d79ea11f and
# 51cc3feb) both failed with `BUILD_PREFLIGHT=FAIL reason=snapshot_dirty`, both
# citing exactly these two paths, regardless of what the uploaded workspace
# snapshot itself contained — proving they are recreated fresh inside the build
# container on every publish, not carried over from the uploaded snapshot.
#   .cache  <- pnpm's local content-addressable store cache, written during the
#              platform's own `pnpm install` step (visible in the build log
#              before the artifact's build script runs).
#   .upm    <- Replit's Universal Package Manager cache, written by the
#              platform's own package-installation step.
# Neither is a build INPUT the release needs; both are always reconstructed by
# the platform itself. `replitignore.test.ts` pins this against the real file.
.cache
.upm

# --- dev-workspace tooling artifacts (added 2026-08-07, PR #83/#84 real-build follow-up) ---
#
# Not build output — regenerated by ordinary use of the live SSH-accessible dev
# workspace itself (a new SSH connection, an editor/agent session, or the
# continuously-running `next dev`/`api-server dev` preview processes), entirely
# independent of what git tracks. Confirmed directly: a fresh SSH connection to
# this workspace regenerates `.config/.vscode-server`; the running dev preview
# processes append to `.replit-preview-logs/*.log`; Replit's own in-workspace
# Agent (or any use of it) recreates `.agents/skills`. None of the three is ever
# read by the build or the production runtime. A real Replit publish attempt on
# 2026-08-07 (build 539f46e5, after PR #85 merged and the workspace was
# reconciled) failed `BUILD_PREFLIGHT=FAIL reason=snapshot_dirty` naming exactly
# `.agents` and `.config`, reconfirmed live in the workspace at the time of this
# fix. `replitignore.test.ts` pins this against the real file.
.agents
.config
.replit-preview-logs

# --- dependency trees (added 2026-08-06) ---
#
# Replit's own description of a publish is "a snapshot of your app's files AND
# DEPENDENCIES", so an installed tree ships unless it is excluded here. A tree
# that ships is unreviewed code that `next build` compiles against, and the
# reviewed lockfile describes what SHOULD be installed, not what IS.
# `dec-pr81-node-modules-excluded-and-lockfile-reinstalled-2026-08-06` resolves
# that by keeping the tree out of the snapshot entirely and having the deployment
# build reconstruct it from the reviewed manifests with a frozen lockfile:
#
#   preflight -> pnpm install --frozen-lockfile -> generate-build-info -> next build
#
# One EXACT path per pnpm workspace package (`pnpm-workspace.yaml`: artifacts/*,
# lib/*, lib/integrations/*, scripts) plus the root. Not `**/node_modules`: a
# directory called `node_modules` somewhere that is not a declared package should
# stop the build for review, not be silently dropped from the snapshot.
# `replitignore.test.ts` pins this list against the real manifest topology, so
# adding a workspace package fails the suite until its rule is considered.
node_modules
artifacts/api-server/node_modules
artifacts/isola/node_modules
artifacts/mockup-sandbox/node_modules
lib/api-client-react/node_modules
lib/api-spec/node_modules
lib/api-zod/node_modules
lib/db/node_modules
scripts/node_modules
